{
  "image_name": "4e5eae619c4a041966e7e38f19aa8654.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a visual overview of major fluorescence microscopy modes, organized by how illumination is delivered and how resolution is improved. It compares point-scanning methods, full-field imaging, and light-sheet approaches, then summarizes several super-resolution strategies.\n\nAcross the top, focused beam methods scan either one spot or many spots through the sample. Conventional confocal/spinning-disk imaging is contrasted with nonlinear 2P/3P excitation and with STED/RESOLFT, where a patterned depletion beam narrows the effective fluorescent region. The middle row shows full-field approaches: wide-field illumination excites the whole area at once, TIRF/HILO restricts excitation near the coverslip using an evanescent or inclined beam, and SIM uses striped illumination patterns to encode finer detail.\n\nThe next row highlights light-sheet geometries, where excitation is confined to a thin plane from the side or at an angle, including lattice or Gaussian sheets and reflected-sheet variants. These schemes reduce out-of-focus excitation compared with standard epi-illumination.\n\nThe bottom row explains resolution concepts. A diffraction-limited point spread function produces blur, while super-resolution methods overcome this in different ways: STED shrinks the emitting spot, SMLM localizes single molecules over time to reconstruct positions or trajectories, and SIM combines multiple patterned images to recover higher spatial detail.",
